WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.580
DUBAI’S FAIRYTALE:
Dubai often likes to tout that they’ve quote

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.170
on quote “escaped oil”.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:11.620
According to their official website, over
95% of their GDP is non-oil based and less

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.559
than 1% of their GDP comes directly from oil.

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:21.350
This makes it seem like Dubai is one of the
most advanced and green cities in the world.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:26.880
And when you consider that the UAE has barely
been around for 50 years, this feat just becomes

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:27.950
even more impressive.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:34.040
But, while I’m sure these GDP statements
are technically true, they’re also super

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.510
misleading.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:38.780
In fact, it’s likely that these pieces of
data were cherry picked to make Dubai’s

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.710
economy look better than it really is.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:47.260
You see, just because oil accounts for a very
small portion of Dubai’s GDP does not mean

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.410
that oil has played a small role in their
economy.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:56.600
Dubai has a deep past with oil and they’re
still very much dependent on oil to this day.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:01.840
Now, this isn’t to Dubai has accomplished
nothing because they definitely have.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.320
They’ve built one of the largest and most
respected hospitality brands in the world.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.260
They’ve got Emirates Airlines, the Burj
Al Arab, the Atlantis, the Burj Khalifa, dozens

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.560
of luxurious malls and restaurants and who
knows what else.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.190
They’ve also made dramatic strides when
it comes to attracting westerners and immigrants.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.430
The UAE has the world’s largest migrant
population in the world in terms of percentage

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.820
coming in at 88%.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.930
And that number is likely weighed down by
the rest of the UAE.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.340
If we looked at Dubai alone, the number is
likely higher than 90%.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.780
On top of all this, Dubai have some of the
loosest economic policies in the world.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.140
They levy 0 income tax on everyday people.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.500
And the ruler of Dubai has gone as far as
saying that “his country would never adopt

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.880
an income tax as a way to tackle the deficit.”

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.450
Given their economic stance, it’s no wonder
why so many high income earners and wealthy

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.520
individuals have decided to move to Dubai.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.610
With all of that being said though, Dubai
is nowhere near as oil dependent as they would

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:04.700
like you to think.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.009
So, here’s the truth behind Dubai’s oil
free fairytale.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:15.670
NOTHING BUT POVERTY:
To really understand why Dubai’s oil free

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.130
narrative is misleading, we have to first
understand how Dubai became “oil free”

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.060
in the first place.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:27.400
Taking a look back, the roots of modern Dubai
date back to year 1822.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:33.390
At the time, Dubai was a modest fishing village
with a population of only 700 to 800 people.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.610
The entire area was controlled by a nearby
town that you’ve probably heard of, Abu

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:38.880
Dhabi.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.840
It wasn’t till 1833 that a tribe called
Al Bu Falasah would rebel against Abu Dhabi

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.310
and seceed, leading to the independence of
Dubai.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:53.050
This movement was led by two men named Obeid
bin Said and Maktoum bin Butti, and these

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.630
two would naturally become the leaders of
the newly formed city.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:01.210
Obeid’s time at the top didn’t last too
long though as he would pass away just 3 years

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.590
after the creation of Dubai.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.800
This gave control of Dubai to Maktoum who
would establish the Maktoum dynasty who are

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.250
the rulers of Dubai to this day.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.910
All was not good though.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:16.599
Dubai was constantly fighting for survival
both economically and politically.

00:03:16.599 --> 00:03:21.250
Dubai was barely making ends meet with their
pearl diving and fishing, and they had no

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.959
money to actually advance the city.

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:28.580
To make things worse, Abu Dhabi wasn’t exactly
a fan of Dubai’s cessation and they were

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.300
looking to reclaim the land if possible.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:36.220
It wasn’t till 1892 that the UK would take
on the responsibility of Dubai’s security,

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.670
enabling Dubai to focus on economic growth.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:43.970
From the very beginning, Dubai was extremely
open to capitalistic ideas.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:48.800
One of the first things they did was establish
a completely free port in 1901.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.410
The port boasted no taxation on imports or
exports, and it also offered merchants protection.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:59.600
This led to a massive influx of merchants
to the area who brought along with them loads

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:00.800
of cargo.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.480
One of Dubai’s biggest merchants was a company
called the Bombay and Persia Steam Navigation

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:06.480
Company.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.680
By 1906, this company alone was trading 70,000
tonnes of cargo through Dubai.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.479
This activity quickly transitioned Dubai into
being a well known port within the area, but

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:21.949
given Dubai’s policy of no taxation, this
wasn’t all that helpful economically.

00:04:21.949 --> 00:04:26.699
Things would take a turn for the worse in
1908 when a gigantic storm would wipe a dozen

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:29.270
pearling boats and a 100 men.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:33.009
Given that the majority of these men were
their family’s breadwinners, their death

00:04:33.009 --> 00:04:36.039
simply pushed Dubai further into the depths
of poverty.

00:04:36.039 --> 00:04:41.270
By this point, Dubai had been independent
for nearly a hundred years, but things were

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:42.849
worse than ever.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:46.229
Atleast, things couldn’t get any worse than
this right?

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:49.309
Well, they could and they would.

00:04:49.309 --> 00:04:54.009
The 1920s would bring with it a new innovation
called cultured pearls which eliminated much

00:04:54.009 --> 00:04:55.860
of the demand for pearl diving.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:02.569
The 1920s also brought with it the Great Depression
which absolutely and utterly destroyed Dubai.

00:05:02.569 --> 00:05:07.669
People started emigrating in droves and the
people who stayed had no other choice.

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:13.310
Really, the only thing that got Dubai through
this period was the innate urge for survival.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:20.270
CLOSE BUT NO CIGAR:
While the state of Dubai was dire to say the

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.330
least, there was a small glimmer of hope.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:27.330
Word was spreading about neighboring kingdoms
striking record amounts of oil and solving

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.259
all of their financial worries.

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:36.069
Having nothing to lose, Dubai would decide
to roll the dice with oil in 1937, but unfortunately,

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:39.719
this gamble wouldn’t pay off atleast not
anytime soon.

00:05:39.719 --> 00:05:45.830
You see, around this same time, the Americans
would find an oil ocean under Saudi Arabia

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:50.180
and they were busy building pipelines and
oil rigs and trade networks.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.280
Not to mention, WW2 was just around the corner.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.389
So, trying to find oil in this tiny little
poverty struck town was no one’s top priority.

00:05:58.389 --> 00:06:03.800
But, eventually after WW2 came to a close
and all of the hype surrounding Saudi Arabia

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:08.949
died down, oil companies would turn their
focus to neighboring areas like Dubai.

00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:17.229
And in 1966, oil would finally be discovered
in Dubai, but the discovery was by no means

00:06:17.229 --> 00:06:18.830
a grand slam.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:25.139
The oil was enough to save Dubai from absolute
despair and start building some infrastructure,

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:28.249
but it was nowhere near enough to convert
them into a desert oasis.

00:06:28.249 --> 00:06:32.039
The same, however, could not be said about
Abu Dhabi.

00:06:32.039 --> 00:06:37.789
Abu Dhabi found oil in 1958, and for them,
it was a grand slam.

00:06:37.789 --> 00:06:44.449
For perspective, the UAE has nearly 100 billion
barrels worth of oil and 95% of this is located

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:46.319
in good old Abu Dhabi.

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:52.210
At first glance, it probably seems like Dubai
got super unlucky and the truth is they did.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:56.249
They’re literally located right next to
all of the world’s largest oil reserves

00:06:56.249 --> 00:07:01.259
including Saudi Arabia, Iran, Iraq, Kuwait,
Abu Dhabi, and Qatar.

00:07:01.259 --> 00:07:05.529
Yet, their little city happens to be in one
of the few locations were there’s relatively

00:07:05.529 --> 00:07:07.289
small amounts of oil.

00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:11.520
While this seems like a curse initially, it
actually turned out to be a blessing because

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.169
it was exactly what was needed for the rulers
of Abu Dhabi and Dubai to put aside their

00:07:15.169 --> 00:07:17.379
differences and join forces.

00:07:17.379 --> 00:07:21.300
From Dubai’s perspective, it would be stupid
to let an ego battle prevent them from joining

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.629
forces with an emirate that controls 9% of
the world’s oil reserves.

00:07:25.629 --> 00:07:30.379
And from Abu Dhabi’s perspective, they were
no longer that salty that Dubai seceeded because

00:07:30.379 --> 00:07:33.289
it looked like Dubai’s bet didn’t pay
off.

00:07:33.289 --> 00:07:37.529
So, it wasn’t too hard for these two emirates
to join forces with a few smaller emirates

00:07:37.529 --> 00:07:40.280
and create the UAE in 1971.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:44.870
Abu Dhabi would become the capital of the
new country and the ruler of Abu Dhabi, Sheikh

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:49.059
Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan, would become the
first president of the UAE.

00:07:49.059 --> 00:07:52.099
This probably doesn’t seem too monumental.

00:07:52.099 --> 00:07:56.439
A couple of neighboring cities got together
and the richest of the bunch became the leaders.

00:07:56.439 --> 00:08:00.289
But, this moment was actually a gamechanger.

00:08:00.289 --> 00:08:06.860
Sure, Dubai had to make some political sacrifices
but this is when Dubai truly won.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:10.909
It wasn’t when they seceded from Abu Dhabi
or when they opened a tax free shipping port

00:08:10.909 --> 00:08:12.979
or when they struck oil themselves.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:18.589
No, it was right here because this marked
the beginning of Abu Dhabi extending significant

00:08:18.589 --> 00:08:23.419
financial support to Dubai that’s still
continuing to this day.

00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:29.139
THE GODFATHER:
Eversince the creation of the UAE, Abu Dhabi

00:08:29.139 --> 00:08:34.690
has acted as the godfather of Dubai funding
dozens of projects, handing out several loans,

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:36.940
and bailing them out on numerous occasions.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:41.580
One of the first projects that Dubai embarked
on after teaming up with Abu Dhabi was building

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.520
a new port called the port of Jebel Ali.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:49.930
This port was built throughout the late 1970s
and would be opened in February of 1979.

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:56.520
Since then, this port has become home to 5000
different companies from 120 different countries.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:01.910
And the port of Jebel Ali currently ranks
as the 11th busiest port in the world.

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:06.910
But, as you might remember, this wasn’t
Dubai’s first shot at building a port.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:10.450
In fact, they tried building a tax free port
way back in 1901.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:16.140
That, however, was wiped out by a simple storm
while the port of Jebel Ali has survived numerous

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.740
explosions, attacks, and natural disasters.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:23.530
The main difference between the two is that
one was backed by oil money while the other

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:24.550
wasn’t.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:29.930
Anyway, shortly after the port was completed,
Dubai set out on their biggest and most successful

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:33.380
project of all time: Emirates Airlines.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:37.990
Emirates was personally funded by the royal
family of Dubai in 1985 and it has since turned

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:41.870
into one of the most luxurious and respected
airlines in the world.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:47.460
But, while people view Emirates as being rich
with their jumbo A380s, Emirates actually

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:53.460
makes less money than Delta, American Airlines,
Lufthansa, United, and even Southwest.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:58.330
So, Emirates is much more of an image piece
than a true profit driver.

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:02.390
And how was this image piece funded, well
I think you know the answer.

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:05.690
Moving on, we have all of Dubai’s vanity
projects.

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:08.280
First, they had the Burj Al Arab in 1999.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:12.080
Then, they had the palm islands in 2004.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:16.910
After that, it was Atlantis in 2008 followed
up by the Burj Khalifa in 2010.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:20.640
And now, we have creek tower, though that
may be cancelled.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.600
As their port, airline, and vacation destinations
grew in popularity, these sectors of the economy

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:28.950
simply outpaced their relatively small oil
industry.

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:35.160
And this is why Dubai is able to claim that
oil only makes up 1% of their GDP, but guess

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:37.510
who comes to their rescue whenever Dubai needs
help.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:40.930
Well, it’s of course their oil rich partners,
Abu Dhabi.

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:45.660
A perfect example of this is 2008 during the
great financial recession.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:50.160
As the global economy cooled down, so did
travel and the revenue that Dubai pulled in.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:55.150
They were no longer able to keep up with vanity
projects like the Burj Khalifa, but luckily

00:10:55.150 --> 00:11:00.280
for them, Abu Dhabi would come to their rescue
with a $10 billion bailout.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:06.260
We should also mention that Dubai has yet
to payback Abu Dhabi, not that Abu Dhabi particularly

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:07.260
cares.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.480
Dubai has consistently just been rolling over
their debt eversince the financial crisis,

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.070
and they currently owe Abu Dhabi $20 billion.

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:17.860
And that’s just their debt to Abu Dhabi.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:21.620
Dubai also has loads of internal and international
debt.

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:27.050
Moody’s and the S&amp;P estimate that Dubai’s
total debt load stands at $79 billion.

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:32.070
Clearly, regardless of whether Dubai is oil
dependent or not, there’s no question that

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:37.480
their debt dependent.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:39.200
THE UGLY TRUTH:
In the end, while Dubai likes to convince

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:43.570
the world that they’re completely self sufficient
and that they’ve been able to beat their

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:47.200
oil dependence, this is simply not the case.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:51.710
Even though oil does not account for a large
portion of their GDP, it’s literally the

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:56.290
only reason the country was able to develop
in the first place and it’s the only reason

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:57.910
that they remain solvent today.

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:02.570
If it wasn’t for Abu Dhabi’s oil bailout,
Dubai would’ve likely went bankrupt back

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:07.860
in 2009 and it’s not clear if the Burj Khalifa
would have ever even opened.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:11.340
This is probably why they decided to name
the building after the ruler of Abu Dhabi

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.750
at the time, Khalifa bin Zayed Al Nayhan.

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:19.850
The truth is that Dubai is very much dependent
on Abu Dhabi’s money, backing, and influence

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:21.680
on the world stage.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:26.030
According to Dubai’s logic, Mecca is oil
independent as well.

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:30.260
After all, Mecca is the world’s second most
lucrative tourist destination in the world

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:34.490
only beaten by Dubai, and they don’t make
much from oil themselves.

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:39.250
Let’s just forget about the fact that Saudi
Arabia backs Mecca with billions of oil dollars

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:40.350
every year.

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:43.750
All that matters is the GDP right?

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:48.820
Obviously, that reasoning doesn’t hold up
for Mecca and it’s no different with Dubai.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.540
Do you think that Dubai is oil independent?

